## The numbers, in one table

| Machine or area | Minimum age |
|---|---|
| Fenced kids area, 70 to 90cc quad | 6 |
| Marked boundary area, single or double seat quad | 12 |
| Open desert, single seat quad | 14 |
| Open desert, double seat quad | 16 for the driver |
| Yamaha Raptor 700cc | 14 |
| Every dune buggy, two and four seat | 14 for the driver |
| KTM 450 dirt bike | 14 |

There is no upper limit on anything. Passengers on double-seat quads and in buggies have no minimum, because they are not driving.

## Why the area sets the age, not the engine

Most listings lead with engine size because it sounds like the important number. It is not.

A 90cc quad on flat, enclosed ground is a different machine from a 350cc quad on a dune face, and the gap between them is the terrain rather than the cubic centimetres. So we split the fleet by **where it is ridden** first: a fenced zone away from the dunes, a marked two-kilometre square with gentle sand, and the open red dunes with a guide.

A child of six can ride safely in the first of those. The same child could not ride the smallest machine we own on an open dune face, and the age rule exists to say so in advance rather than at the base.

## No licence, and why that surprises people

Nobody needs a driving licence on our guided routes. The land is private, the routes are supervised, and a fourteen year old can legally drive a [dune buggy](/dune-buggy-dubai/) here while being eight years away from driving a car on Sheikh Zayed Road.

We ask for photo ID for the booking record. That is a paperwork step, not a test, and it applies to the person booking rather than to every rider.

Two follow-up questions come up often enough to answer here. An international driving permit changes nothing, because no permit is being checked. And a UAE resident visa is not required either: visitors and residents book on the same terms and pay the same price.

What people should check before they travel has nothing to do with licences at all. Read the exclusions on your own travel policy, because desert sports sit on a lot of standard exclusion lists and that is the sort of thing people only look at afterwards.

## Age is a floor, not a clearance

This is the part that gets left out, and it is the part that actually decides the day.

Every rider gets a briefing, a helmet fitting and a warm-up on flat sand before anything else happens. The guide is watching during that warm-up. If somebody is over the age, has booked the machine, and clearly cannot control it, they get moved to a smaller one or into the boundary area.

That happens most often on the [KTM 450](/ktm-dirt-bike-dubai/), where the gap between the age floor and the real requirement is widest. It is a desert enduro with a clutch and gears, and guests who have ridden a scooter sometimes book it expecting a step up rather than a different sport.

It is not a good conversation to have at the base and we would rather have it on WhatsApp. If in doubt, book the smaller machine. Moving up on the day is easy and nobody has ever been annoyed about it.

## How mixed-age groups actually work

Families almost never arrive with everyone in one bracket, and the fleet is built around that rather than in spite of it.

A common session has the youngest in the fenced area on a 70cc, two teenagers in the boundary square on single seats, and the adults out in the open dunes. Same base, same session, three different areas. Nobody waits in a car park and nobody is put somewhere unsuitable so that the group stays together.

The other route for younger children is the passenger seat. Double-seat quads and every buggy carry a second person at no extra cost, because the price covers the machine rather than the head count. A nine year old who cannot drive anything yet can still be in the dunes with a parent at the wheel.

## What to send us before you book

Ages, group size, date and whether anyone has ridden before. That is enough for us to come back with the machines, the riding areas and the total in AED.

[Quad prices](/quad-bike-dubai/price/) start at AED 75 for thirty minutes in the kids area and AED 89 for adults in the boundary area. Buggies start at AED 300 and the KTM 450 at AED 500. Every one of those is per vehicle. Hotel pickup inside Dubai is free.

We have run this base since 2020 and taken more than 17,000 guests out, and the bookings that go wrong are almost always the ones where nobody mentioned an age until everybody was standing in the sand.
